{
  "id": "Compound::DB00338 - Gene::495",
  "verbalization": "Omeprazole, a proton pump inhibitor, is linked to Atp4a through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that Omeprazole, through its metabolite Esomeprazole, binds to Atp4a. This binding is supported by multiple paths, all of which share a common pathway involving the 5-Methoxy-2-{[(4-Methoxy-3,5-Dimethylpyridin-2-Yl)Methyl]Sulfinyl}-1H-Benzimidazole molecule, which serves as a common ancestor for Omeprazole and Esomeprazole. The presence of this molecule in the pathway indicates a potential molecular mechanism by which Omeprazole influences Atp4a. The relationship is further reinforced by the observation that Omeprazole causes physiological effects such as mucosal atrophy and carcinoid tumour formation, which are mediated through Esomeprazole. These findings collectively suggest that Omeprazole exerts its effects on Atp4a via a complex, multi-step process involving its metabolite and the enzyme Atp4a.",
